      "source": [
        "# Takeaways:\n",
        "\n",
        "***Data Availability Bias*** \n",
        "- Trained model on apartments in ~15 different Manhattan neighborhoods with listing price between $3k-$7k. Given this price range typically fields studio or 1 br apartments in Manhattan, the best-deal results were skewed towards the less common 2-3 bedroom apartments that met this critera.\n",
        "- Expectedly, # of bedrooms commanded the highest weight for all models. Almost 60% of listings do not include Square feet, yet the feature had a second highest coefficient weight for the 4 models.\n",
        "\n",
        "***Features Used***\n",
        "- Street Easy provided ~50 distinct possible amentities. Some of these were rare and showed true signal (elevator and gym had highest weight coefficients), but others were related or even irrelevant to renters (city view)\n",
        "- I tested to see whether it would be better to manually reduce (combine) features prior to modeling or let LASSO / RIDGE detect this naturally. LASSO using the aggregated features showed best results in dataset used. Results were close across all four models. Appears that reducing the total number of features helped the LASSO model sift through the noise.\n",
        "- I one-hot encoded categrocial neighborhoods codes; however large neighborhoods like UES and Chelsea can distort results. By ignoring the actual cross blocks, apartments across the street from each other in different neighborhoods (according to Street Easy) would be scored separately.\n",
        "\n",
        "***AI Created Features*** \n",
        "- I used Gemini 2.5-Flash to review the listing images. See below for the prompt I used. Although the results lead to correlated features, using AI lead to a systemmatic review and ranking for over 5000 images."
      ]
    },
